  First, the project state, because it shapes everything else. The repo is one commit and one file:

  - [38;5;153mindex.html[39m — a static page with [38;5;153m<h1>Tasks</h1>[39m and an empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m. No JavaScript, no data model, no server, no
    build tooling, no dependencies, no tests.

  So there are no tasks, no users, no notion of a task "changing," and no channel to notify anyone through. "Notify
  users when tasks they care about change" presupposes all four. That's not a reason to stop — but it means I need to
  know what I'm actually designing against before I can ask useful questions about the notifications themselves.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this repo is the real app (in which case notifications depend on a task system that doesn't exist
  yet, and we sequence that) or a stub standing in for something that already exists elsewhere (in which case I should
  be designing against that system's real constraints, not inventing them).

  - [3mRepo is the whole app[0m — we decompose: the task model and change events come first, notifications build on them.
    Honest sequencing, but notifications are not the next thing you get.
  - [3mReal app exists elsewhere[0m — I design against its actual stack, auth, and storage. Much better spec, but I need to
    see it or have you describe it.
  - [3mGreenfield, design both together[0m — one spec covering a minimal task model plus notifications as its first consumer.
    Fastest path to something whole, at the cost of a wider spec.

  I'd recommend telling me it's greenfield if that's the truth, and letting me scope the task model to the minimum the
  notification design actually needs — inventing a plausible task system in my head and specifying against it is where
  this goes wrong.
